From 5d71966373ad357a610bfae44535728c980a0061 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:05:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] The pictures left out before the first
 one of a video is shown are not counted as skipped

The player information counted one or more pictures as skipped right
after the start of every video ("skipped 1" for Avatar, 5 for Hokum on
an RTX 4080 SUPER with an LG C5), while Hades' own summary, which leaves
the frames around a start out (CLostFrames), said that everything
worked, and kodi.log said nothing of them.

VideoPlayer starts its clock when the first picture of a video is ready
("CVideoPlayerVideo - in step with the clock 0 ms after its first
picture was ready", 00:04:43.166), and the renderer shows its first
frame at the first refresh after it is set up for the new mode: 126 ms
later in that start (00:04:43.292, "the first frame of the video went
out 300 ms after the Dolby Vision signal"), when three pictures at
23.976 Hz had come due. PrepareNextRender() shows the latest picture due
and leaves the others out, counting each but the first as skipped
(m_QueueSkip), as it does for a picture that is late while the video
plays. Upstream Kodi does the same.

Holding the clock until the output shows a frame would move the sound
and the timing of the first frame of native Dolby Vision, which are
right as they are: the picture shown is the one that is due. So what is
done does not change; the pictures left out before the first one shown
are counted apart (StartSkips.h, CStartSkips: from PreInit() to the
first picture chosen), GetSkippedFrames() gives those skipped while the
video plays, and kodi.log says once a video how many came due before
the first frame and how late the picture shown was.
---
